* Fri Sep 25 2026 Jeremy Melanson <1080872+zish@users.noreply.github.com> - 1.0.0-1
- First package. Teaches the host's SELinux policy the three identifiers vold's
  AppFuse mount asks for, so StorageManager.openProxyFileDescriptor() works and
  apps whose documents are generated rather than stored can open them.
- Three identifiers, not two: both types are Android-only and so is the SELinux
  user `u`, which is the part easy to miss -- u:object_r:fusefs_t:s0 is invalid
  on a Fedora host with a type Fedora certainly has, so declaring the types
  alone still returns EINVAL.
- Declarations plus exactly one allow rule, each proven load-bearing by removal.
  A first draft's four container_runtime_t rules and wide file access were all
  redundant: assigning the file_type and filesystem_type attributes is what
  makes the base policy's existing rules apply to the new types.
- Loaded from a boot-time unit rather than only from %%post, because on an
  rpm-ostree host a scriptlet runs against the compose and the policy never
  reaches the booted system -- the lesson waydroid-ext-backlight learned on its
  first real install.
- The loader verifies rather than assuming: it writes each context vold needs to
  /sys/fs/selinux/context and reports what the kernel refuses to parse. semodule
  exiting 0 is not evidence the fix is in force, and a parse failure produces no
  AVC at all, so ausearch stays silent while dmesg carries the reason.
